  },
  {
    question: 'What is the difference between LLumar ATC and Low-E film?',
    answer: "LLumar ATC is primarily a solar control product optimized for summer heat rejection. LLumar Low-E is a year-round film that also retains indoor heat in winter — the better choice for Philadelphia's four-season climate and older single-pane glass.",
  },
  {
    question: 'Can LLumar film be applied to double-pane insulated glass?',
    answer: 'Yes. LLumar makes film series specifically rated for insulated glass units (IGUs). Selecting the wrong film for double-pane glass can cause thermal stress. Window Film Philadelphia always evaluates glass type and orientation before recommending a film.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is LLumar film the same as Vista film?',
    answer: 'Both LLumar and Vista are Eastman Performance Films brands manufactured on the same platform with shared R&D. LLumar targets commercial architectural applications; Vista is positioned as the premium design-forward line. The products differ in specific series and aesthetics.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Does LLumar ATC film interfere with Wi-Fi or cellular signals?',
    answer: "No. The ATC Series is non-metallized, so it does not attenuate Wi-Fi or cellular signals — an important advantage in Philadelphia's dense commercial buildings. The Reflective Privacy Series uses a metallized coating and may affect signal in high-glass-density facades.",
  },
  {
    question: 'Will LLumar film qualify for LEED or energy credits?',
